# Original teaching notes. Each worked example is executed, not hand-transcribed.
guide_content <- list(
  `descriptive-statistics`=c("Describe the observations before modelling", "Select numeric traits and, if appropriate, a grouping variable. The examples cover overall and grouped summaries, boxplots, histograms, Q\u2013Q plots and Shapiro\u2013Wilk normality checks.", "Report the number of observations, mean, sample SD and observed range for each trait or group. State units. SD describes dispersion of observations; it is not the SE of a mean.", "Normality of raw observations is not the same as normality of model residuals. A nonsignificant Shapiro\u2013Wilk result does not prove normality. Missing observations must first be reviewed in Data Preparation."),
  `compare-means`=c("Report the difference with its uncertainty", "Choose a one-sample, equal-variance two-sample, Welch or paired t-test. Paired data must have the two measurements aligned within each experimental unit.", "The result contains the explicitly named contrast, difference estimate, SE, df, t, p-value and 95% confidence interval for that same difference. The independent contrast subtracts the second factor level from the first; paired contrasts subtract right from left.", "One-sample results express the mean minus the hypothesized value. Welch allows unequal variances; pairing must follow the design rather than data ordering alone. All tests here are two-sided. A variance test is diagnostic, not an automatic rule for choosing a t-test."),
  `correlation-regression`=c("Separate association from prediction", "Choose a numeric response and one or more numeric predictors. The examples show simple and multiple regression, using one reviewed common observation set for the model and correlation matrix.", "Report coefficients with SE, t and p-value, R\u00b2, adjusted R\u00b2, residual SD and the fitted model. Inspect residuals, linearity and the relevance of predictors. Correlation-matrix tests are unadjusted pairwise Pearson tests.", "Correlation does not establish causation. Perfectly dependent or constant predictors are rejected. The small teaching dataset illustrates the mechanics; it is not enough to establish a reliable predictive model."),
  `data-preparation`=c("Start with the experimental unit", "A row should represent a recorded experimental unit. Choose the trait columns and the identifiers that together identify that unit. Replication-wide tables can be converted to long format.", "Inspect the validation summary and repeated-identifier table before fitting a model. Repeated identifiers may be subsamples, not additional independent replications. The exclusion ledger is part of the analysis record.", "Review omissions explicitly. This tool does not decide whether an outlier is erroneous and does not impute missing observations."),
  `design-analyzer`=c("Randomize before planting", "Choose the design, treatment counts, replication counts and a reproducible seed. The examples cover CRD, RCBD, factorial CRD/RCBD, split plot, strip plot and augmented RCBD.", "The fieldbook records treatment allocation, plot identifiers and the seed. Record the actual field orientation and retain the original allocation before entering responses.", "Recording row and column positions are planning coordinates. Confirm the physical field orientation and randomization restrictions before using them for a spatial analysis."),
  `alpha-layout`=c("Allocate resolvable incomplete blocks", "Specify the genotype count, entries per block, number of complete replicates and seed. Block size must divide the genotype count; the agricolae alpha-design restrictions also apply.", "Each genotype should occur once in every replicate. Keep the replicate and incomplete-block identifiers with every response measurement.", "This is a layout generator. It does not establish that the chosen field geometry removes spatial variation or guarantee a particular efficiency gain."),
  `power-planning`=c("Plan replication from an effect worth detecting", "Supply anticipated group means, residual SD, significance level and target power. Choose balanced CRD ANOVA or two independent groups.", "The recommended count is rounded up per group and power is recalculated for that integer design. The sensitivity curve shows how power changes with replication.", "The calculation assumes independent normal observations and a common variance. It does not include blocks, split plots, clustering, attrition or multiple-primary-outcome adjustment."),
  `crd-rbd`=c("Compare treatments using the experimental error", "Enter one row per treatment and one column per replication. Use CRD for unrestricted randomization or RBD for complete blocks. Treatment identifiers must be unique.", "Report the design, treatment and error df, SS, MS, F and p-value. Treatment means have model-based SE and t confidence limits. State LSD, Duncan or Tukey explicitly; LSD and Duncan do not provide the same familywise protection as Tukey.", "Shared letters indicate that the comparison did not reject a difference; they do not prove equivalence. An insignificant omnibus test does not establish equality. No automatic model-based outlier deletion occurs."),
  `factorial-design`=c("Retain the interaction and explain the simple effects", "Provide one row per factor combination, in A-major/B-minor order: A1B1, A1B2, then A2B1, A2B2. Set the actual number of levels and choose CRD or RBD.", "The full factorial model remains the inference model. Report the interaction with main effects and interpret marginal means in that context. Tukey pairwise tables and compact-letter displays are provided; the additive ANOVA is a separate descriptive comparison.", "The software does not choose an additive model from an interaction p-value. Main effects can obscure a strong interaction. Confirm the row-to-factor mapping shown in the long-data table."),
  `split-plot`=c("Respect the whole-plot and subplot errors", "Provide Rep, MainPlot, SubPlot and Value, with one observation per complete cell. Custom columns can be mapped explicitly. The classical tables require a complete balanced design.", "The classical ANOVA uses Error(a) for main plots and Error(b) for subplots and interaction. Comparison summaries report SE(d), not SE(m). Comparisons spanning main plots use an approximate Satterthwaite df. Mixed-model outputs are separately identified.", "Half-LSD bars are comparison aids, not confidence intervals. Keep the interaction in the model. Fixed and mixed analyses can differ at boundary variance estimates; inspect diagnostics rather than selecting whichever gives significance."),
  `strip-plot`=c("Use three appropriate error strata", "Supply a complete Rep \u00d7 FactorA \u00d7 FactorB layout. Both treatment factors are randomized in strips, making this different from a split-plot design.", "Report factor A against Rep:A, factor B against Rep:B, and the interaction against the residual interaction error. The estimated-means model includes these strata. Tukey corrections apply within each displayed comparison family.", "Do not analyze a strip plot as an ordinary factorial CRD. Unbalanced or duplicated cells are rejected in this workflow."),
  `augmented-trial`=c("Adjust unreplicated entries using replicated checks", "Include at least two checks once in every block and each test entry exactly once. Select the checks explicitly; do not infer check status from a treatment name.", "Report adjusted means and the appropriate comparison standard errors. The ANOVA is sequential, with blocks fitted before adjusted genotype effects. Differences from the average adjusted check mean are descriptive contrasts.", "Unreplicated test entries do not acquire independent replication through adjustment. DAU Fisher LSD comparisons are unadjusted for multiplicity; prioritize scientifically planned comparisons."),
  `incomplete-block`=c("Recover genotype information across small blocks", "Provide Genotype, Rep, Block and numeric traits. Every genotype must occur once in each complete replicate; incomplete blocks must be equal-sized and smaller than a replicate.", "Replication is fixed and incomplete blocks within replication are random. Choose fixed genotypes for adjusted means or random genotypes for BLUPs. Report the model, variance components and any boundary diagnostics.", "The simple balanced-RCBD entry-mean heritability and advance formulas are deliberately unavailable here. Conditional BLUP-effect intervals hold fitted variance components fixed."),
  `spatial-field`=c("Model spatial variation using observed field coordinates", "Map genotype, replication, numeric row, numeric column and response. Coordinates must identify unique plots, with at least four distinct rows and columns.", "SpATS fits a PSANOVA surface with fixed replication and fixed or random genotype. Report the specification and genotype estimates with their model-provided SEs. Inspect the field map, fitted surface and residual map together.", "Residual RMSE is in-sample fit, not proof of predictive improvement. A model reaching its iteration limit is not a validated fit. Coordinate scales and field geometry are part of the scientific model."),
  `pooled-anova`=c("Combine seasons with a stated inferential scope", "Provide Season, Rep, Treatment and Value. Every treatment must be represented in every season. The current model treats seasons, treatments and nested blocks as fixed.", "Report seasonal error MS and df alongside the combined sequential ANOVA. The residual median-centred Levene calculation is a diagnostic; it does not automatically authorize pooling.", "Inference is conditional on the observed seasons. For a random sample of environments, use a mixed-model approach with an appropriate genotype-by-environment structure."),
  `pooled-split-plot`=c("Combine split-plot trials across years", "Supply Year, Rep, MainPlot, SubPlot and Value. The same complete treatment structure and replicate count are required in every year.", "The mixed model contains fixed year and treatment interactions, with random Year:Rep and Year:Rep:MainPlot intercepts. Report Satterthwaite df, variance components, estimated means and the specified Fisher LSD contrasts.", "Fisher LSD contrast p-values are unadjusted. Marginal comparisons across years average interactions. The saturated treatment structure is retained; boundary fits and convergence warnings require scientific review."),
  `met-stability`=c("Separate mean performance, interaction and stability", "Map genotype, environment, replication and a numeric trait. Supply all genotype-environment means for AMMI/GGE; missing cells are never silently imputed. Choose whether high or low values are desirable.", "The examples include AMMI, GGE plots, WAASB/WAASBY, BLUP-based harmonic indices, rank-sum summaries and a two-trait batch. WAASBY uses 70% performance and 30% stability. Report centring/scaling and trait direction with biplots.", "Harmonic indices need positive predictions in every environment. Batch top-ten summaries are descriptive rank summaries, not MGIDI or a formal genetic selection index. An unavailable method remains unavailable with a diagnostic; substitute formulas are not silently used."),
  `genetic-variability`=c("Distinguish plot and entry-mean heritability", "Use replicated single-environment genotype observations. Genotype is random and replication fixed. Multiple selected traits share the reviewed complete-case set.", "For balanced RCBD, genetic variance equals (MSgenotype \u2212 MSerror)/r when the REML solution is interior. Entry-mean H\u00b2 is \u03c3g\u00b2/(\u03c3g\u00b2 + \u03c3e\u00b2/r); conditional plot H\u00b2 uses \u03c3g\u00b2/(\u03c3g\u00b2 + \u03c3e\u00b2). Report the scale explicitly. Predicted means average fixed replication effects equally.", "CVs require a positive trait mean. Genetic advance is a model-based expectation at the chosen selection intensity, not observed realized gain. BLUP intervals describe conditional genotypic effects, not uncertainty of the absolute predicted mean."),
  `multi-trait-selection`=c("Make the ideotype and weights explicit", "Supply one row per genotype and numeric trait columns. Specify traits where lower is desirable, the weights and the selected percentage; confirm the direction choices before running.", "Weighted scores average signed standardized traits. MGIDI instead uses a factor model and distance to an ideotype. Report the chosen method and weights. The selection cutoff uses ceiling(n \u00d7 percentage/100), retaining all ties.", "The methods are not interchangeable. Selection differentials describe the input matrix, whether it contains observed means, adjusted means or BLUPs; they are not promised genetic gain."),
  `pca-diversity`=c("Explore multivariate relationships on a defined scale", "Use one row per genotype and at least two varying numeric traits. Traits are centred and divided by their sample SD before PCA and Euclidean Ward.D2 clustering.", "Report eigenvalue percentages, loadings and scores. The biplot overlays unit loading vectors on score coordinates; read numerical loadings for exact contributions. The dendrogram describes the chosen standardized trait distance.", "Cluster membership is exploratory, not proof of genetic ancestry or population structure. Principal-component signs may reverse between equivalent decompositions without changing the solution."),
  `combining-ability`=c("Estimate effects for a clearly defined parental set", "Choose a balanced crosses-only line \u00d7 tester trial, or Griffing Method II with parents and one F1 per nonreciprocal cross in every replicate. Parent identifiers and replication are required.", "Report fixed GCA and SCA effects, residual-model SEs, confidence intervals and Holm-adjusted effect tests. The diallel implementation derives uncertainty from residual covariance rather than the package's replication-MS standard-error field.", "The effects describe the tested parents. They are not estimates of additive and dominance variance in a randomly sampled population. Reciprocal effects, other Griffing methods and multi-environment combining ability are outside this implementation."),
  heterosis=c("State the reference and the direction of improvement", "Use a balanced replicated trial containing both parents, separate hybrids and optionally a commercial check. Select the reference entries and whether a lower response is better.", "Heterosis is 100 \u00d7 (hybrid/reference \u2212 1). Report mid-parent, observed better-parent and standard-check comparisons separately, with mean differences, SEs and Holm-adjusted difference tests. Percentage intervals use the delta method.", "Percentage uncertainty is unstable near a zero reference. Better-parent intervals condition on the observed selected parent. Negative heterosis can be desirable for a lower-is-better trait; statistical significance alone does not establish usefulness."),
  `generation-means`=c("Fit generation means with a declared parameterization", "Provide one mean and positive sampling SE for P1, P2, F1, F2, BC1 and BC2. The analysis assumes these six means are independent.", "The fitted equation is m + a\u00b7d + b\u00b7h + a\u00b2\u00b7i + ab\u00b7j + b\u00b2\u00b7l. The exported coefficient matrix is authoritative: BC1/BC2 use \u00b11/4 for the j coefficient. Report three-parameter joint lack of fit and A\u2013D scaling tests before interpreting the saturated six-parameter estimates.", "Some publications define the additive \u00d7 dominance coefficient as 2ab; their j estimate is half the ab-convention estimate here. Do not compare coefficients across conventions without transformation. The full six-parameter fit has zero lack-of-fit df; supplied SEs are treated as known sampling uncertainty.")
)
